## Results
Each of the 20 attacks was run twice (40 runs per level). Replies were
scored automatically by checking for the secret values, then verified
by reading every flagged reply.

| Level | Attack success rate |
|---|---|
| 1. Naive (no security rules) | 15% (6 of 40) |
| 2. With rules | 0% (0 of 40) |
| 3. Strict prompt | 0% (0 of 40) |
| 4. Strict prompt + output filter | 0% (0 of 40) |

Naive bot, by category: role-play 20%, extraction 20%, indirect 20%,
instruction override 0%.

## Findings
- Without security rules, the chatbot leaked confidential data in 3 of
  20 distinct attacks: the admin password inside a role-play story, a
  customer balance on a direct question, and the full customer record
  when an instruction was hidden in a spell-check task.
- Adding explicit security rules reduced successful attacks to 0% on
  this set.
- Levels 3 and 4 showed no further improvement because level 2 was
  already at 0%, so the extra value of the strict prompt and the output
  filter is not demonstrated by these attacks.

## Limitations
- Small attack set (20 attacks); results are indicative only.
- Temperature was 0, so repeated runs gave nearly identical replies and
  the two runs per attack are not independent samples.
- One model (gpt-4o-mini); other models may behave differently.
- The scorer uses string matching. An early version counted the customer
  name alone as a leak and overstated the naive rate (20%), which I
  corrected after reading the replies by hand. It could still miss a
  leak in a disguised form.
- Prompt injection has no complete fix; 0% on these attacks does not mean
  the chatbot is secure.
- All secrets are fake, and only my own chatbot was attacked.